    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q23-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The sum of three positive numbers in geometric progression is 7. Given that the product of the first and third term is $\\frac{16}{9}$, find the largest of the three numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{16}{3}$.\n\nThe middle term is the positive square root of the product of the end terms.",
          "worked": "Let the three terms be $x,y,z$. Since they are positive and in geometric progression,\n$$\\begin{align*}y^2&=xz=\\frac{16}{9}\\\\y&=\\frac43\\\\x+z&=7-\\frac43=\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe end terms are the roots of\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2-\\frac{17}{3}t+\\frac{16}{9}&=0\\\\(t-\\tfrac13)(t-\\tfrac{16}{3})&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThe three numbers are $\\frac13,\\frac43,\\frac{16}{3}$, in either order. The largest is $\\frac{16}{3}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q23-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Four positive numbers are in geometric progression. The sum of the first two is 2 and the sum of the last two is 50. Find the largest of the four numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{125}{3}$.\n\nDivide the two pair sums to find $r^2$.",
          "worked": "Write the terms as $a,ar,ar^2,ar^3$, with $a,r>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}a(1+r)&=2\\\\ar^2(1+r)&=50\\\\r^2&=\\frac{50}{2}=25\\\\r&=5\\\\a&=\\frac{2}{1+5}=\\frac13\\\\ar^3&=\\frac13\\times5^3=\\frac{125}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $r>1$, the fourth term is the largest.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q23-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Five positive numbers are in geometric progression. The sum of the first and last is 17, and the product of the first and last is 16. Find the largest of the five numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$16$.\n\nThe end terms are $1$ and $16$; a positive geometric progression is monotonic.",
          "worked": "Let the first and last terms be $x$ and $y$. Their sum is 17 and their product is 16, so they are the roots of\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2-17t+16&=0\\\\(t-1)(t-16)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the end terms are $1$ and $16$. With a positive common ratio, the progression is increasing or decreasing, so its largest term is an endpoint. The largest number is $16$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      }
    ]
  },
